Here's a walkthrough for running your collection with Postman, in the order that matches your flow (the plan-limit sequence depends on it).

Phase 1: Local run (do this first, at home)

1. Prepare the environment

bash
npm install
npm run seed      # should log "MongoDB connected" + admin user and genres created
npm run dev       # http://localhost:4000

Open http://localhost:4000/health. You should see {"status":"success","message":"API is up"}.

2. Import the collection

Postman → Import → select postman/music-playlist-api.postman_collection.json.

3. Set the collection variables

Click the collection name → Variables tab.
Set prod_base_url to http://localhost:4000 (temporarily).
Check that admin_username / admin_password match ADMIN_USERNAME / ADMIN_PASSWORD in your .env.
Click Save.

4. Run the whole collection with the Collection Runner

Right-click the collection → Run collection (or the Run button).
Keep the folder order as it is: Auth → Genres → Playlists Core → Users → Playlists After Upgrade → Tracks → Cleanup.
Leave "Persist responses" / "Keep variable values" enabled so access_token, playlist_id and genre_id carry over between requests.
Iterations: 1. Delay: try 100-200 ms (helps with your rate limiter on /auth).
Click Run.

5. Read the results  

Every request should show green tests. Look at the Failed tab first if something is red.
Common causes of failure:
429 Too Many Requests on /auth: the strict rate limiter is triggering. Temporarily raise the limit for testing, or add a delay between requests.
Duplicate user errors on re-runs: the register request was already used. Either run Cleanup (which deletes the user) or make sure the register request uses a random username ({{$randomInt}} or {{$timestamp}}).
Seed not run: the admin login will fail with 401.
Playlist limit flow broken: if you run a single folder alone, the 4-playlist → 403 → upgrade → 5th sequence will fail because earlier variables aren't set. Always run the full collection.

6. Manual request
"Upload Cover Image - Success (200) [MANUAL]" will fail in the runner, since it needs a real file. Either run it by hand once (Body → form-data → key image, type File) or skip it in the runner by unchecking it.

7. Re-runnable state
Make sure the Cleanup folder leaves the DB clean, so you can run the whole collection again from scratch without conflicts.

Phase 2: Deploy to Vercel + MongoDB Atlas

1. MongoDB Atlas DONE

Create a free cluster → Database Access: create a user + password.
Network Access: add 0.0.0.0/0 (Vercel uses dynamic IPs).
Connect → Drivers: copy the connection string, replacing <password>.

2. Push to GitHub (make sure .env and node_modules are in .gitignore). DONE

3. Vercel

Import the repo in Vercel.
Settings → Environment Variables: add everything from your .env (MONGODB_URI with the Atlas string, JWT_SECRET, CLOUDINARY_*, ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_PASSWORD, etc.).
Deploy (or vercel --prod from the CLI).

4. Seed the production DB
Run the seed locally, but pointing at Atlas:

bash
MONGODB_URI="<your atlas string>" npm run seed

(On Windows PowerShell: $env:MONGODB_URI="..."; npm run seed.) Otherwise, the admin login on prod will fail.

5. Check prod health
Open https://<your-app>.vercel.app/health.

Phase 3: Run against production
In the collection Variables, set prod_base_url to https://<your-app>.vercel.app, and Save.
Run the full collection with the Runner again.
Fix anything that behaves differently in prod. Typical suspects are missing env vars, the Cloudinary config, and Atlas network access.
Phase 4: Prepare the submission

The assignment requires one folder + two files in a ZIP/RAR (max 40 MB, due 1/10/2026, 21:00):

Source code folder, without node_modules.
A file with the URL of the published API (e.g. url.txt).
The exported Postman collection (JSON).

Export tip: the instructor's comment in the PDF notes that exporting can lose the variable's value. Before exporting, put the Vercel URL in both the Initial value and Current value of prod_base_url, then click Save, then Export → Collection v2.1. Open the JSON and search for prod_base_url to confirm the URL is in there.

Quick checklist before submitting
 Full collection passes against the Vercel URL
 prod_base_url has the remote URL in the exported JSON
 Every endpoint has tests for each status code implemented
 README updated (and mention any AI usage, since the assignment requires citing it)
 ZIP is under 40 MB with no node_modules
 You can explain every part of the code for the defense (Oct 5-8)

If you want, tell me what fails when you run it and I can help debug.

Incorporating new instructions into the walkthrough plan.

Those notes add a few things to the checklist. Here's how they change the plan.

1. Edge-case tests (limits)

Your current flow (4 playlists as plus, 5th gets 403, upgrade, 5th succeeds) is only half of what the professor asks for. Add:

Plus boundaries: the 4th playlist succeeds (201), the 5th fails (403). Testing both sides of the boundary catches off-by-one bugs.
Premium beyond the limit: after the upgrade, keep creating until you have 11 playlists. The professor's "11 documents" example fits well here, because 11 is also one more than your default page size of 10. One loop covers both the "premium has no limit" check and pagination: GET /playlists?page=1&limit=10 returns 10 items, and page=2 returns 1.
Plus users who delete: a plus user at 4 playlists deletes one, then can create again. This checks you count current playlists rather than a lifetime counter.
Upgrade edge cases: upgrading when already premium should return an error (the letter says it requires being on plus), and an admin hitting the upgrade endpoint should get the error you decided on.

You can write the loop as a repeated request in the runner, or use postman.setNextRequest() in a pre-request/test script, or simply add 11 requests to that folder.

2. Mongo doesn't validate references

Check that your services explicitly verify referenced documents exist:

POST /playlists and PATCH /playlists/:id with a genre ID that doesn't exist must return 404 or 400, not save.
A malformed ObjectId (genre: "abc") should return 400, which Joi or a CastError handler can cover.
POST /playlists/:id/tracks with a bad :id should also be handled.
Deleting a genre that's in use should be blocked (you already have this). Deleting a user should cascade their playlists (also covered).

Add a Postman request for each of these with a test for the expected status code.

3. Initial data in Atlas
Your npm run seed covers admin and genres. Consider also seeding one regular user and a few playlists, so the graders can explore the API without running the whole collection first.
Run the seed against Atlas (the MONGODB_URI="..." npm run seed command from before).
Take a backup with mongodump --uri="<atlas uri>" --out=./backup, or export from Atlas/Compass. Redo it now and then as the professor suggests, but don't put the backup in the ZIP unless you want to.
Make sure the Cleanup folder doesn't delete the seeded data.
4. Postman documentation and test style
Randomized values: use a pre-request script such as pm.collectionVariables.set("rand_user", "user_" + Date.now()), or {{$randomInt}} and {{$randomFirstName}} in bodies. This also fixes the duplicate-user problem on re-runs.
Test names and descriptions: rename tests to neutral wording, e.g. "This test validates that the response status is 403 when a plus user creates a fifth playlist", not "Here we test...". Add a short request description saying what is expected and why.
Publish: click the collection, open Overview → View complete documentation → Publish (top right), and save that public link. The link in the professor's message is his own example, so you need your own.
5. Cleanup before submitting
bash
# find leftover console statements
grep -rn "console\." --include=*.js . --exclude-dir=node_modules

# find unused dependencies and files
npx depcheck
npx knip
Remove console.log from the app code. The seed script is a gray area: the professor said to delete logs, so I'd keep at most one or two in seed.js or drop them too. If you do keep them, be ready to explain why.
Delete unused files, dependencies and env vars (for example, anything left from a Redis setup or other experiments). Keep .env.example with variable names only, never real secrets.
Exclude node_modules from the ZIP. The project should run both locally and on Vercel.
6. What goes in the ZIP
The whole project folder (no node_modules, no .env).
A .txt file with both URLs: the Vercel API URL and your published Postman documentation URL.
The exported Postman collection as JSON, with prod_base_url set to the Vercel URL.
Revised order of work
Add the missing tests (limit, 11 playlists, bad references, random data).
Run everything locally in the Collection Runner.
Clean up the code and unused files.
Deploy to Vercel + Atlas, seed Atlas, back it up.
Run the full collection against production.
Publish the Postman docs, export the JSON, and write the URL .txt.
Zip it and submit before 21:00 on 1/10.

If you paste your playlists service and controller, I can check that the reference validation and the plus/premium limit logic handle these edge cases.

on this step:
5. Read the results
request: error: localhost request not supported

That error isn't coming from your API. "localhost request not supported" is Postman itself refusing to send the request, so your server never saw it.

Why it happens

The Collection Runner in Postman for Web (the browser version) can't reach localhost, because the browser sandbox blocks it. Local requests only work from:

The Postman Desktop app, or
The browser version with the Postman Desktop Agent installed and selected.
Fix

Option A: use the Desktop app (simplest)
Download and open the Postman desktop app, import the collection there, and run it again.

Option B: use the Desktop Agent in the browser

Install the Postman Desktop Agent.
In Postman web, click the agent selector at the bottom right of the window (the satellite/globe icon) and choose Desktop Agent.
Re-run the collection.

Then check that the server is actually running (npm run dev) and that http://localhost:4000/health works in your browser.

Why only that request shows up as failed

Your first request in the list failed at the network level, so it appears in Failed. If the other requests are not listed as failed, they probably did run fine, or the run stopped early. Check the run summary at the top: it shows how many requests ran, how many tests passed and how many failed. If the count is low, re-run and watch whether it stops after that first request.

Side note

Once you deploy to Vercel and set prod_base_url to the https://...vercel.app URL, this problem disappears even on the web version, since it's no longer localhost. You still want the local run done first, so you can catch bugs before deploying.

If it still fails after switching to the Desktop app or the Agent, paste the error text and I'll look at it with you.

